A||||86||DrDave||Caddy V8  Cams||||1||
Z||000000||DrDave||12-07-2001||08:52 PM||||    Like most of those contemlating the 4.9L Caddy engine, I wasn't happly with the 4800rpm limit.  (altho, lets face it, there's more than enought power there to get you into real trouble.)   I feel happy to announce that I've found a source of Cams.<BR>    Delta Cams in NorthWestern Washington has a large selection of cams for the 4.9.<BR>    they can grind cams that allow stock ECMs, modified Caddy ECMs, require modified aftermarket or Chev ECMs.  And some that exceed the capacity of ECMS.<BR>    1-800-562-5500.    He prefers voice as opposed to Email.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mach10||12-08-2001||02:32 AM||||Oh, you are the MAN!!!! Sweet find, dude!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000002||rockcrawl||12-08-2001||08:42 AM||||I'll take four! I guess I'll be giving him a call on Monday. Thanks Dave.||||reg||10
Z||000003||DrDave||12-08-2001||05:27 PM||||     I am excited too, that cam choices exist.  It has been pointed out to me in no uncertain terms, that the Caddy does quite well as is.  Prior to my ordering a cam, I'm going to do much more research.   I did in fact have to install 3 cams in the 406(in my Land Cruiser) befor I found the right balance of what torque at what Rpm I really wanted.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||12-08-2001||10:39 PM||||Yes, I'm very happy with the way the stock cam works with the automatic. I'd swap it out for some overall power gains, but I don't think the range of power needs to be any different. It doesn't need any power above 4500 with an auto because it never revs that high. I'd like to do the next one (or three) with a five speed.||||reg||7
Z||000005||DrDave||12-08-2001||11:48 PM||||     George says that with a manual that it's hard to hook up.  I figgured that looking at the different grinds, then profiling them I might be able to make it easier to hook up and the result would be shifting the power up a little in the rpm band.   Theory is neat, fact is better.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ferrari_cdn||12-09-2001||01:22 AM||||You mean, hard to hook up, as in difficulty to not spin the tires?  Come now, it can't be that hard to not spin the tires with a manual.  Just release the clutch slower so you accelerate better.  Or is that a bad idea since you burn the clutch that way?  I got a 4spd and I am planning on the 4.9L as well.  If it is a headache to not spin the tires, what do you recommend, switch to auto or stick with manual and just keep a garageful of tires?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ICEFierosig.gif"> AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000007||DrDave||12-09-2001||03:28 PM||||     I suspect the fellas I'm listing to, are speaking from a racing/autocrossing viewpoint.  So... If ya want to accelerate rapidly, feathering the clutch would be Real touchy to accomplish.    ||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||12-09-2001||07:29 PM||||No, he means eeeease it into first, ok, we're in gear now, rooling.  edge onto the gas... and 275lb-ft spank you instantly. BwappSCREEEEE. STOP!  Ok, still rooling in first, try a little throttle again, SCREEEEE!<BR>no good.<P>Manual trannys like a motor that's at least a little closer to dreaming of being flat (let alone peaky) <P>Re-ground cams may make a world of difference, and really wake up this engine (they may also throw rods through aliminum like hot knives through butter, I don't know how strong or balanced the 4.9s internals are...)<P>I'd imagine that if you *babied* a car with a standard 4.9v8 and a 4:10 4-speed... that it'd be driveable... with sticky tyres... but it won't be a heck of a lot of fun for me, just when things get intresting, time to shift again.<P>PS: feathering clutches is a good way to make them explode...<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ferrari_cdn:<BR><B>You mean, hard to hook up, as in difficulty to not spin the tires?  Come now, it can't be that hard to not spin the tires with a manual.  Just release the clutch slower so you accelerate better.  Or is that a bad idea since you burn the clutch that way?  I got a 4spd and I am planning on the 4.9L as well.  If it is a headache to not spin the tires, what do you recommend, switch to auto or stick with manual and just keep a garageful of tires?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||Mach10||12-10-2001||12:55 AM||||Oh, come ON, now... It won't be THAT bad  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Opening up to 1/2 throttle won't strip the pavement off. Feather the throttle, not the clutch... but yes, it IS a stump-puller. <P>A cam that knocks some of the bottom-end grunt off, and moves it up the RPM band would be usefull for both the auto/manny simply due to less traction. At least, that's how I see it. Personally, I'm looking for a useable powerband from 3000rpm to 5500. not interested in 6000+, myself. <P>But with those 4.10s... Ugh... I'd say lose the 4:10, and nab yourself a 3.33 or less if you can find it.<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000010||Raydar||12-10-2001||09:40 AM||||I remember reading elsewhere (other thread or mailing list) that the 4T60 that comes with the Caddy engine has a 2.84 final drive ratio.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>The 88 4 cyl auto tranny is also a 2.84. If you are looking for an automatic, but don't want to replace your axles and such, this might be the ticket. You'll lose the overdrive, but I suspect that the acceleration will still be startling, without sending the tires up in great clouds of smoke.<BR>I wonder what the safest maximum RPM is on a 4.9, if the cam and valve springs were replaced. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfixit58||12-10-2001||11:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>"I wonder what the safest maximum RPM is on a 4.9, if the cam and valve springs were replaced. "<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, I wonder about that too. That doesn't mean I'm not going to do the swap (heck, the motors are just sooo cheap), it's just that I would like to know the limitations.<P>I dismantled a 1990 4.5 in the bone yard the other day and was looking at the aluminum rocker stud rack. It looks like a heavy valve spring may tax it beyond it's capabilities. <P>One idea I had was to buy long studs and allow them to protrude through the valve cover then use an lock nut on each side. The valve cover then become a valve girdle. Of course leaks would have to be addressed but at least you'd gain some additional support.<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <P><B>JUST ADDED: </B>1988 GT w/ T-Tops, 5-Speed, and Performance Sound.||||reg||3
Z||000012||rockcrawl||12-10-2001||04:44 PM|||| If you can't pull out without spinning the tires, you need to practice driving a bit more. It doesn't matter if you have 600 hp and 600 ft/lb at 1500 RPM, you should still be able to get the car rolling quickly without tire spin. It's no different than driving a front engine- rear wheel drive car, you need to regulate the power via the throttle. Camaro owners need to deal with this every day. Nobody says you need to have the pedal floored right off the line. I understand that when racing you want to get all of the power to the ground all the time. Choosing the correct gear ratio is a good way to accomplish that. A car that has the ability to spin the tires with such ease is actually quite enjoyable to drive. Maybe not the fastest at the track or the most economical to operate, but fun to drive. Who cares if you need to shift at 4500, the car is still pulling hard the whole time, and when you do shift you don't need to wait for the engine to wind up before it's making power again. The power is always there. I have a Fiero with a stock V6 mated to a 4.10 four speed, it will burn tires all day long, but nobody ever says the 2.8 has too much low rpm power. On the other hand I have a Fiero with a 4.5 and 2.84? automatic that does 0-60 in 5.2 seconds and can barely squeak the tires at the line. I just read George's post on the Cadero forum, and it sounds like he and I are thinking alike. I do think it would be nice to shift some of the power toward the higher rpms, but I don't think it's a problem the way it is. <P>The valve cover won't make a very good girdle. It's very thin and cracks easily. Also take a look at the way they are held down. There is a large rubber gromet around each bolt, and nothing to keep the valve cover from moving around except this rubber. I'm not sure a girdle is necesary anyway, there are lots of engines that use the same style rocker arrangement, the 3800 is one of them. I have never heard of any failures or problems. You already have the bridges tying the two rockers together.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Mach10||12-10-2001||05:51 PM||||Is the 4800 redline due to valve float? Doesn't sound like it to me. If it makes power right up to ECM cut-off, I'd say the springs are working just fine. DO the valves float at 4.8k?<P>I heard that the motor starts making a huge racket at that speed... Think I'm DEFINITELY going to have the rotating assembly balanced. I'm not asking for much. 6k cut-off, power to at least 5500... Anyone have any suggestions? <P>The planned rebuild will have the following, assuming I can afford it in reasonable time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>-Rotating assembly balanced<BR>-Stiffer springs if needed. CUSTOM if needed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><BR>-Ported heads<BR>-Slightly more aggressive cam from aforementioned providers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000014||rockcrawl||12-10-2001||07:54 PM||||I just took my Cadero out an gave it a thrashing. The automatic keeps it below 4500 at all times so I kept it in 2nd gear for testing. It pulls strong from idle right up to 5000, at which point the top end starts making a hideous racket. I did that about three times before I decided I'd better stop before I break something. I know for a fact that the '94s were limited to 4500 RPM by the factory ECM, and I can see why. ~4800 seems to be the mechanical limit.<BR> <BR> SBC valve springs are the correct size for the 4.9, you just need to choose the right pressure and  bind height. Delta Cams supplies springs with the cam. I'm thinking that just adding stiffer springs will make quite a difference with the stock cam.<P> It shouldn't take much to ballance the bottom end of a 4.9 because they are already ballanced. Just tighten up on the GM toerances a bit. ||||reg||10
Z||000015||lou_dias||12-10-2001||08:27 PM||||could the racket be from worn down lifters allowing some gap at high rpms?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ferrari_cdn||12-10-2001||08:50 PM||||I am to assume that even at high speeds, 100 kph (62 mph) it will STILL pull strong when floored due to high torque?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ICEFierosig.gif">  AIM: CanadianFiero<p>[This message has been edited by Ferrari_cdn (edited 12-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||rockcrawl||12-10-2001||10:04 PM||||The lifters are hydraulic roller type, wear is not really an issue. I think it's just the skimpy valve springs with no dampers. I believe a simple spring upgrade will do the trick. <BR> <BR> I've never driven this car and wished there was more power. It always pulls. It accelerates so smoothly that before you know it your doing 80mph and still accelerating strong. There is no need to change anything if you're using an automatic, this engine was designed to be used exclusively with an automatic, and it works very well with one. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||cadero2dmax||12-11-2001||01:17 AM||||Can I jump in here for a moment?<P>My comment about putting the power down was exactly what Rockcrawl said - in a competitive environment. And as (again) he so aptly put it, for daily driving, drive it as a Camaro driver does his car, and you will be just fine.<P>I chose the Th125 for the Challenge car for a couple reasons. It should be faster at the strip. The 4.9/Isuzu makes for a fun ride in town, you can break the tires loose at will, yet the car is very manageable to drive. It took me about 1/2 hour to get used to the clutch/torque (keep in mind I also have a 24V Cummins turbo diesel 5 speed, so instant torque is not completely foreign to me). Speaking of foreign, the riceboys have a hard time using their NOS at every light :-)<P>But to get a proper launch, the throttle MUST be feathered, or you simply go up in smoke. Yes, like the RWD cars. But after having experienced the Quad 4 where you ran the engine to 3500, dump the clutch/floor the throttle at the same time - for a 1.7 60' time - I know how a Fiero can launch if set up right. I think a 2.84 ratio is the way to go. I chose the TH125 because it is about 25 pounds or so lighter than the Cad, and because of cost restraints (the stock axles can be used, etc.)<P><BR>Even with the stick, the stock 4.9 is just about right for a fun, economical ride (not many other V8's can offer 24mpg - if driven sanely - and be as bulletproof as this motor is). It also has torque right where you need it for exiting a corner, making it a good autocrosser - in stock configuration. Mach 1.0 said he wanted usable power from 3500 to 550RPM with a 4.10 tranny. It is very obvious he has never driven a 4.9, he would have usable power from idle to 4500RPM - a lot more usable powerband than his SBC sounding numbers. and why limit you top end speed/RPM while hurting your launch (you would not be able to smoothly launch a 4.9 with that trans. By the time you were able to eeeeaaassse the clutch out and feather the throttle, little Honda boy would be in second gear at the 60' stripe).<P>I also would like to take a moment and share an old timers experience with camming a motor. When I returned from my first tour in Viet Nam in 1966, I bought a brand new (first NEW car I had bought to that point) Plymouth with a hi-per 383 and automatic. My first action was to put a lopey cam and glasspaks on it (remember, this was in the 60's and I was young and foolish with some $ to spend). Before the car had 15,000 miles on it, it had an Isky "5 cycle" cam, Hooker fenderwell headers, and dual short ram 4bbl quadrajets from an earlier 413 engine. <P>Stock, the car was in the low 14's, and with this very lopey, header pinging turn heads at the intersection configuration I had built at the base hobby shop, I was turning high 14's. I lost almost a second in the 1/4 trying to better that motors performance. And the cam hurt it the worst. Like I said, it looked and sounded impressive, but it wasn't nearly as good a performer in reality (it is really amazing how a little noise makes one think they are going faster).  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The ending of this story is that I put about $3000 into this motor later on( a LOT of money in the 60's), having a pro build it for me. It ended up putting out almost 450hp, and got into the low 12's - a very respectable time for a big and heavy daily driver back then. <P>Getting back to the present, don't misinterpret what I said about camming this 4.9 motor. There is a lot of improvement that could be done. It will never give SBC or 302 numbers, but done right, this motor can be a lot more impressive. But don't JUST cam it, have it pro built, concentrating on the intake/exhaust. In order to do it right, I am afraid you will have as much $ invested as you would a good SBC build, and not have near the performance or numbers. I contend if that is where your heart takes you, you need to do an Archies kit - you will be $ ahead at the end, and have a  better performing motor to show for your time and money. <P>Drive a STOCK 4.9 Fiero first, spend some time with it (it is obvious that most responants haven't driven one). Then write about what you think. Jon and I are the only ones with running examples of this combo that have responded to this topic, I believe. Please hear what we both are saying here - it doesn't need another grand or two thrown at it - it is great right out of the box. And light to light, it will stay with all but the most exotic TransMaroStangs.<P>The beauty of this motor is that it is very reliable (it is not unusual to see 200,000 mile stock Cads still out there with no problems or major maintenance done), extremely simple for an engine swap (almost bolt-in as swaps go - every bit as easy as my Q4 conversions were), and probably the best bang for the buck out there.<P>In my opinion, the KISS theory is the best approach with what we are doing with this motor (Keep It Simple, Stupid!!).<P>G  <P>||||reg||2
Z||000019||Ferrari_cdn||12-11-2001||01:54 AM||||Uh, I think you misunderstood Mach.  Mach and I are both planning doing a 4.9L swap, and I have the manual tranny with the 4.10, and he has the auto.  He is going to keep the auto.  I think he meant that I should lose my 4.10 and get another tranny.  (He drives a 86 SE V6 and I drive a 84 SE 4cyl).<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ICEFierosig.gif"> AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000020||Mach10||12-11-2001||03:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadero2dmax:<BR><B>Can I jump in here for a moment?<BR>...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Quite right... I don't have much experience with this motor... For my purposes, I am tossing it into an automatic Fiero. As Ice said, the 4.10 is in HIS car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>What I was talking about is that I want the powerband shifted up about 1000rpm. The current powerband seems to be 2500 onward. I'd like a little less bottom and a little more upper. Based on what I've seen and heard, I *should* be able to do this with a mild build. <P>However, coming from people who've DONE the swap is making me reconsider. I think I'll take your advice to heart and just slap it in bone-stock. I should probably learn the motor, and actually figure out what I want to change, and go from there  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>||||reg||12
Z||000021||cadero2dmax||12-11-2001||04:24 AM||||Actually, there is more pull at idle than there is with a lot of larger engines, the 2400RPM is where the PEAK torque is (about 1500RPM sooner than the SBC or Mustangs). Then it is almost flat to 4500. That is the beauty of this engine, and the part that most people miss when comparing "numbers".<P>As I mentioned, I have a Cummins Dodge. Now this engine has 235hp and 440lbft of torque, with a redline at 3400 RPM. Yet you will see these trucks passing on the interstates, while pulling heavy loads, at illegal speeds (and just loafing along at 2300 or 2400 RPM doing it). And getting close to double the mileage than a higher horsepower, higher revvin' V10 pulling the same load at the same speeds. <P>Does the massive torque and relatively low horsepower slow these monsters down? No, they handle themselves quite admirably, and last forever doing it. <P>Well, think of the 4.9 as the diesels of the Fiero community. Drive them hard, they can take it. But don't let the "numbers" throw you. You will have a quick, economical ride with that V8 growl that you can break tires loose on at any time, yet drives quite civilized until you want to wake it up by pushing the loud pedal.<P>And all with limited RPM and stock cam!!<P>G  ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Eric_PA||12-11-2001||06:48 AM||||I have got to jump in here, I had a ride in Rockcrawls 4.5 automatic. I must say I was very impressed. I wish I would have gone with a 4.9 auto instead of spending all the money for a 3800 performance rebuild. In my opinion, they work great with the automatics and don't let the horsepower numbers scare you away like they did to me. That torque really moves the fiero. The only thing I have to say is !!I WANT ONE!! ||||reg||6
Z||000023||cadero2dmax||12-11-2001||07:14 AM||||Thanks, Eric, for certifying what Rockcrawl and I have been trying to tell the world,<P>Our society is really into the "My Dog is Bigger" way of thinking. Why, it is not comprhensible that smaller is faster in the automotive world. For a 200hp to stay up with 300hp, why somebody MUST be fibbing - -.<P>Of course, even seasoned gearheads are so tuned into "horsepower" numbers by the Madison Avenue types that they forget the adage that is as true today as it was when it was said - - horsepower sells, torque wins races". Torque that comes on as strong and as early as the Cad 4.X series will leave a host of much more "capable" cars in their dust.<P>Thanks again for the validation.<P>G||||reg||1
Z||000024||Alex4mula||12-11-2001||09:58 AM||||I think I'll go this way on my car. Couple questions;<BR> Which is the best source for mounts for this engine?<BR> Can I put a SBC carb to the 4.9 with good results? <BR>I was thinking on a Holley Street Avenger and then forget about the FI stuff by now.<BR>TIA<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000025||rockcrawl||12-11-2001||08:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>....yet drives quite civilized until you want to wake it up by pushing the loud pedal...<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> That is a perfect description. I could let my mother drive the car and she'd never know it wasn't stock. When it shifts into OD at highway speeds, all you hear is wind noise. One of Eric's first comments was about how smoothly it runs. But open up the throttle and it really roars.<P>Jon||||reg||1
