Z||000034||87fastback||12-13-2001||12:19 AM||||Sorry for jumping in this thread so late, but I hadn't been watching it.<P>I don't personally have a 4.9, but I have ALOT of practical experience with Vette's and Camaro's.  If the 4.9 does in fact have roller lifters as Rockcrawl mentioned, then yes valve springs will do the trick.  But not just stiffer ones, as a stiffer spring will lead to early cam failure and so forth.  The real key is to use multiple springs.  When I was working with Chevrolet a small block with stock springs and a high profile cam would make a horrible racket at high RPM.  Add roller lifters would help from a horsepower standpoint as they have less resistance on the cam lobe, but the real success came from a double or triple valve spring.  What I mean by this is springs within a spring.  The outer one may be stock height, wire size etc . . . but the inner springs were just smaller diameter.  This would eliminate valve float without adding a whole bunch of extra valve seat and lifter pressure.  In essence the double springs will keep the rocker arm postioned on the valve stem properly, which IS valve float!  Bigger stiffer springs are NOT the answer, try to find the proper double valve spring for this application.  Both Crane and Comp Cams has ALL the specs on their springs in the catalog.  You will just need a 4.9 head in front of you to do the sizing.  Sorry I can't help with that.<P>Anyways, in short I think that as Rockcrawl is describing the TRUE shortcoming of the 4.9 is valve float.  Swap the springs and you will effectively alter the ability to rev higher, but it will STILL be dependant on the cam profile.  Girdles and all that other fancy stuff is NOT needed, that stuff is just a waste of money for a rally racer.  Put that money into suspension mods to better control traction of the car.||||reg||1
Z||000035||87fastback||12-13-2001||12:27 AM||||I forgot to add one other thing.  I really consider Rockcrawl and Cadero2dmax as the biggest experts when it comes to this swap.  I am by no means speaking from experience with this engine, as I have never worked with one.  I can only apply what I know of others.<P>But as far as alot of the speculation that I have seen on this forum as well as the Cadero forum, I am somewhat dissappointed in the way that alot of these wanabe's have jumped in offering solutions.  And please don't get me wrong here, I just think that unless we actually "OWN" one of these engine and have practical experience with them we can only "speculate" as to what may remedy some of the shortcomings.<P>Lets just ALL keep that in perspective, and yes that includes me!  When I have a 4.9 sitting in the back of my 87 then, and only then can I speak from experience!<P>Thanks Rockcrawl and Cadero2dmax for all your "true" experience in this swap!  Keep it coming!||||reg||1
Z||000036||Smoooooth GT||12-13-2001||12:55 AM||||HHmm....  4.9L is soundingbetter alllll the time!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/vroom_5.gif">        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooooothgtsig_9.gif">                <BR>      <P> <A HREF="http://www.thefibersmith.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefibersmith.com</A> <P>Technical  Support (903) 825-7744 Steve<BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||6
Z||000037||cadero2dmax||12-13-2001||02:13 AM||||87 Fastback<P>Thank You for sharing your thoughts about the valve springs. I will have to try that on the Challenge car, if I can find some "cheap" springs.<P>BTW, I am no expert on anything internal on an engine. I have had the engine built on every one of my racecars. <P>Installing, "engineering", and "thinking outside the box" are some of the things I do well, though. I was the first one to use a Wilwood brake master cylinder for a clutch MC on the Fiero, for example. My Q4 conversion was running at the Pueblo, CO racetrack a year before ACE came out with their conversion kit (Gee, they are located just south of Pueblo - - amazing coindidence, don't you think?).  Rodney can verify that I was the first one to successfully use a FWD Getrag with the internal slave and Thorsen-Gleason in a Fiero.<P>I have experimented with a lot of different things on the track or autocross course (with an occasional blast down the 1/4 for ProSolo start practice    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) <P>My forte, however, is in making a Fiero handle. I have raced them for years and have the credentials to back that up. But the engines and electrics are Rockcrawl's bag. We just complement each other well, don't you think? My weakness is his strength, etc.<P>Anyway, thanks for the flowers! <P><BR>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to:  <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821</A>  <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)<p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000038||Mach10||12-13-2001||03:10 AM||||Do I have to do anything special to get signed up on the cadero forum? I've done the Join steps... I assume that I'll be added once someone(?) looks at it? ||||reg||1
Z||000039||Bluemagic||12-13-2001||03:31 AM||||After reading what every body had to say about the 4.9,the SBC,the 3800 I am the proud owner of a 4.9powered fiero notchback and until you own one of these properly done you are missing fun fun fun.I started out to put a 350 SBC but after doing my maths on power vs engine weight I went with the aluminum 4.9,I did a rebuild from bottom up bearings,melling oil pump, arp rod bolts,rings and finallydid massive head work porting and polishing double springs and had a Chet Herbert  cam specially ground for this motor msd ignition module and last but not least a nitros works 125hp bottle and this car is the baddest I have ever driven.<BR>Having the low rpm is not even in  question with tons of torque it pulls till you get scared of going any faster it just boggles the mind and with the nitro coming in at 3000 rpm for get it ,I have a 90 corvette and there is no comparison what so ever so I am a witness that the POS 4.9 when done right will run with any thing on the road.<P>------------------<BR>Keith<BR>85 SE Fiero 4.9L Caddy <BR>345hp||||reg||14
Z||000040||mrfixit58||12-13-2001||07:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Bluemagic:<BR><B>After reading what every body had to say about the 4.9,the SBC,the 3800 I am the proud owner of a 4.9powered fiero notchback ... I went with the aluminum 4.9,I did a rebuild from bottom up bearings,melling oil pump, arp rod bolts,rings and finallydid massive head work porting and polishing double springs and had a Chet Herbert  cam specially ground for this motor msd ignition module and last but not least a nitros works 125hp bottle and this car is the baddest I have ever driven.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Keith,<P>What are you using to control the engine? Are you using the MPI system or are you converted to throttle body? You combo sounds great.<P>Roy<P>||||reg||1
Z||000041||87fastback||12-13-2001||09:28 AM||||Quote by Bluemagic "...I did a rebuild from bottom up bearings,melling oil pump, arp rod bolts,rings and finallydid massive head work porting and polishing double springs and had a Chet Herbert cam specially ground for this motor msd ignition module and last but not least a nitros works 125hp bottle..."<P>Hey Bluemagic, what did you end up using for valve springs, brand, part number, etc...  Also wondering which NOS system you used?  I personally think from what I have heard from others who have "successfully" completed this swap have agreed that this engine is more than capable of producing enough power for a great ride.  I used to have a 95' Vette and I was actually quite dissappointed in it's overall performance.  It did sing all the way up to 6500 RPM, but that was just valvetrain doing the work which is nothing that can't be improved with the 4.9.<P>When it comes to engine building there are SO many factors involved.  I have been involved in everything from boats to motorcycles to quads and ALOT in between.  Build the engine for "what it will be used for"!  I have seen SO MANY people in the past build up an engine with all the roller cams, high-lift cams and all that just to put in the daily driver and are VERY dissappointed in the performance.  It should be obvious why.  Shifting power through the power band really is not the answer for the 4.9, "UNLESS" you are desgning it for THAT purpose!  For a daily driver which I believe most of us are aiming for, the stock 4.9 with maybe a cam and valve-spring change and maybe a port match would really suffice for regular use.  I think a whole bunch of people may have misinterpreted what some others who have done the swap have mentioned as far as the 4.9 shortcomings.<P>As far as the small black thing Bluemagic, I too had contemplated the LT1 or even and earlier small block with maybe a TPI or something.  The parts are cheap and plentifull, add-ons are available and best of all any competent technician can work on it.  BUT, have you ever compared the differences in price of changing over to this engine versus the 4.9?  Plus look at the weight difference.  Sure we could all go out and put Northstars in our Fiero's, but who has that kind of money to spend - I have a family!  Anyways, for the most bang for your buck and for about the same price as a rebuild on a stroked 2.8 or even a 3.4 swap go with a 4.9 or even the 4.5.  I have been convinced and just located a 93' 4.9 to mate up with my Getrag.||||reg||1
Z||000042||cadero2dmax||12-13-2001||08:35 PM||||I have a couple things to say.<P>I will guarantee you there are one of two scenarios involved here.<P>First is that the 300+ horsepower was arrived at by Joe's methods - mathematics. Hmm, this oughtta give xxhp, and that xxhp more. I sincerely doubt that it was dyno'd, on either an engine or chassis dyno - - just imaginary numbers.<P>The custom intake to make the 4.9 breath well enough to do all that would have been in the thousand dollar price range by itself!<P>Oh, and the NOS - - what is the engine without that?? Maybe 250hp?? How big of a shot was that again???<P>The second scenario is the reality one. IF that engine is what it is said to be, it cost $, - - - REALLY BIG dollars to put that engine together. For an equal amount of $, I would BET that I could build a ZZ4 or 383 and have a hell of a lot more car. A Fiero that would put this one down by better than a second.<P>As a side note, if you put the Caddy auto behind the N*, you have a powertrain that weighs MORE than an equivalant SBC setup.<P>See the Fiero Racing List, that thread is going on right now!<P>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to: <BR>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821 <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)||||reg||1
Z||000043||Bluemagic||12-14-2001||11:53 AM||||I have read all the remarks and I learn more from you guys about Fieros first to begin withI have done a lot of conversion over the years eg:351W Toyota pick up,Jaguar350 SBC,Volvo 350 SBC, Pinto 351W,and a 500 Cu Cadillac powered Ford pickup they all provided me with a sense of satisfaction but my Fiero 4.9 took the prize.Even with out my nitro(pn-113-10110 125hp by Nitrous Works)$368  it still hauls from the line being the torque monster it is and when I activate my bottle it is all over,I have made it a passion of mine to take on the best on the streets eg Camaros,Mustangs 5.0& 4.6L forget the rice burners and the onlycar thar ever gave a run for the money was a 99 Supra twin turbo not off the line but on the top end and he did not believe it when I saw him later of course we were two old farts trying to regain our youth so it only proves you can go fast with limited resource and lo of knowledge and Bubbajoe,Rockcrawl I have learned so much by putting the info together and the rest of the crew so wit that I am getting carried away got to go make $5 to pay the mortgage so take care .<BR>ps:the springs were done at my local machine shop with the head work.<P>------------------<BR>Keith<BR>85 SE Fiero 4.9L Caddy <BR>345hp||||reg||6
Z||000044||cadero2dmax||12-14-2001||08:34 PM||||But Blue Magic - - you still haven't told us (truthfully) how much all that cost??<P>G||||reg||1
Z||000045||Bluemagic||12-15-2001||01:58 PM||||As far as cost is it is relatively low for me here in Miami I have been fortunate to have friends who have <BR>machine shops ,paint and transmission shops so we barter with each other plus we go to the drag meet also and this gives meI guess an edge but I cant put a dollar amount  on the project and if I guess I would be lying but I wil give it a try.<BR>Donor car $250<BR>Reuilt tranny Free(brter)<BR>Engine rebuild and machine work$400<BR>mini Starter Free<BR>Nitro Kit $375<BR>Seats recovered (barter)<BR>Brake rotors and calipers$50<BR>Caliper and hub machining$45<BR>and it goes on so I consider my self fortunatewith tons of junk yards all within a 15 mile radius  move to miami may we can start remanufacturing them.<P>------------------<BR>Keith<BR>85 SE Fiero 4.9L Caddy <BR>345hp||||reg||1
