Z||000056||AnimalGT||12-18-2001||02:08 AM||||What are the advantages of the Allante intake?  It's a MPFI system (with longer intake runners), right?  How does it stack up vs. the PFI system; and are the ECMs completely different or related?<BR>  Could 1.6:1 roller rockers from a SBC work in this motor?  Anybody done it? <BR>TIA,<BR>Scott<P>PS- Does anyone know how well the bottom ends of these motors hold up to abuse?   Could they handle 6500 rpm shifts for 50k miles?  (Providing a valve train can be found that will let the motor rev that high.)<p>[This message has been edited by AnimalGT (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000057||mrfixit58||12-18-2001||08:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AnimalGT:<BR><B>What are the advantages of the Allante intake?  It's a MPFI system (with longer intake runners), right?  How does it stack up vs. the PFI system; and are the ECMs completely different or related?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>From what I've read, the Allante intake will add about 20 HP. I assume it'll also add some MORE bottom end torque (which I understand that in a Fiero, it's already hard to keep the tires from going up in smoke using the standard MPI intake).<P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>  Could 1.6:1 roller rockers from a SBC work in this motor?  Anybody done it? <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The 4.x motors already come with 1.6:1 rockers from the factory.<P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>PS- Does anyone know how well the bottom ends of these motors hold up to abuse?   Could they handle 6500 rpm shifts for 50k miles?  (Providing a valve train can be found that will let the motor rev that high.)<P>[This message has been edited by AnimalGT (edited 12-18-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Using the stock ECM, the motor is limited to 4500 RPM. Rockcrawl has reported the above that, the valves seem to float. One of the problems may be the weak single coil spring. He has also reported that a SBC spring should fit. However, given the limitations of the stock ECM, adding stiffer springs may do nothing.<P>Regarding reliability, I can only state that I've not heard from anyone about a 4.5 or 4.9 motor being worn out. I took apart a 100,000+ mile 4.5 motor in a u-pull-it yard as a "learning experience" and was astounded that I could still see the 60-degree cross hatch pattern in the cylinder walls. I didn't have time to tear apart the bottom end.<P>Your question about the bottom end reliability is valid. However, until someone steps up with real world experiences to share, that question will remain. Possible someone with a carbureted motor or Rockcrawl with his custom ECM (once the bugs have been worked out) can shed a little more light on that question.<P>However, you question about the "6500 rpm shifts for 50k mile" is a little over the top. You'd be hard pressed to find a SBC motor that could handle that. Even if the Caddy motor could handle it, how many transmissions do you want to replace?<P>Roy<P>||||reg||1
Z||000058||brharris27370||12-18-2001||09:31 AM||||Has anyone used or checked into a rev kit for the 4.9 like AFR has for the SBC. <A HREF="http://www.airflowresearch.com/hydrarev.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.airflowresearch.com/hydrarev.html</A> <P>Judging from their dyno charts it would increase the redline by 500 rpm without sacrificing bottom end by counteracting valve train weight.||||reg||3
Z||000059||V8Dreamer||12-18-2001||09:44 AM||||I'm gonna have to follow up with what Eric said, I've been in Rockcrawl's cadiero many times now, and I just went for the best ride yet last night....I couldn't believe how quiet and smooth it was when we left his house.....and then he stepped on the pedal a bit, and lemme just tell you, both the sound and feel are just totally awesome.  It may not smoke tires, but I'll definately vouch for that 0-60 in 5.2 time.  The best part is that you could take this car out on the highway for a couple of hours and cruise along at just over 2g at 65mph!<P>-Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000060||Mach10||12-18-2001||03:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by brharris27370:<BR><B>Has anyone used or checked into a rev kit for the 4.9 like AFR has for the SBC. <A HREF="http://www.airflowresearch.com/hydrarev.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.airflowresearch.com/hydrarev.html</A> <P>Judging from their dyno charts it would increase the redline by 500 rpm without sacrificing bottom end by counteracting valve train weight.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>All it really is, is a lifter kit... Won't solve the problem of the feeble valve-springs, or the ECM-limiter. I can't see it making a hell of a lot of difference. Most hydraulic lifters are good to about ~6000rpm anyway... I'd save the money, and go for a cam... Even *IF* they made these things for a 4.9.<P>||||reg||12
Z||000061||rockcrawl||12-18-2001||05:10 PM||||I'm almost embarrassed to show these pics, the engine is really dirty. I've been driving the car a lot with the lower inner fender skirts removed. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dirtycad.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dirtycad2.jpg"> <P>A pic of what the trunk looks like after removing the bottom half for exhaust clearance.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/flattrunk.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/blue2m8.jpg"> <P>Jon||||reg||7
Z||000062||Slammed Fiero||12-18-2001||05:38 PM||||Keep em comming , This looks like a Good conversion for a poor college student who has other expenses , namely a girlfriend.<P><BR>Does the trunk bottem have to be cut out , or is this just the best route for a free flow exhaust?<P>Hmmmm.<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000063||Raydar||12-18-2001||05:45 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>A pic of what the trunk looks like after removing the bottom half for exhaust clearance.<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/flattrunk.jpg">  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So *that's* where the exhaust system goes.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Was wondering about that.<P>That's a very clean looking installation, though. <BR>I want one.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000064||rockcrawl||12-18-2001||06:10 PM||||The specs on the valve springs are as follows:<BR>outside diameter- 1.35"<BR>free length- 1.95"<BR>pounds to compress to 1.73"(valve closed)- 68-76<BR>pounds to compress to 1.35"(valve open)- 214-232<P>A Chevy LO3/LO5 5.0/5.7 from the same year has the following specs:<BR>outside diameter- 1.45"<BR>free length- 2.03"<BR>pounds to compress to 1.7"(valve closed)- 76-84<BR>pounds to compress to 1.25"(valve open)- 194-206<BR>damper free length- 1.86", approx 4 coils.<P>Look at those numbers closely. Even with a longer stroke, the chevy springs apply more pressure when the valve is closed, with less pressure at max lift. I'm not sure if those specs are with the damper installed or without, I assume it's without. A quick look at the Summit and Jeg's catalogs revealed a huge selection of springs that will fit the 4.9. Many of the listings show complete specs to help you make your choice. Prices are ranging from $40 to over $200 (ouch!) for a set of 16. <P>The stock rockers are 1.6:1 ratio. If you need to have roller tip rockers ($ ouch again!$$) you will need to tap the rocker rail for studs and chevy rocker might fit. The engine already has roller lifters, I don't have specs on them.<P>I also have complete valve and cam specs if anyone is interested.||||reg||1
Z||000065||rockcrawl||12-18-2001||06:19 PM||||You wouldn't need to cut the trunk for exhaust, I just prefer to. With the trunk cut out, there is plenty of room for a cat and any muffler you choose, and all the 2.5 or 3" pipe you need. It keeps the exhast system simple, rather than tryint to change the exhaust outlet on the engine and trying to follow the stock Fiero routing. If you want a cat and a muffler, you will most likely end up losing the bottom of the trunk. I actually like the flat trunk, I have done a few cars like that. If you get a flat, you'll end up putting it on the passenger's seat for the ride home. <P>On this car I used a quiet single outlet muffler for the ultimate sleeper effect.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/49exhaust.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
