Z||000069||PBJ||12-18-2001||07:58 PM||||I think a few of the swaps are stick shifts, I know one of bubbajoe's has a stick that the has pictures of his flywheel (before and after pics) I believe he said a place in Toronto mod'ed his of about $100 cdn, but he may have had connections???  <P>My swap is coming along, have the engine (93 4.9), I have the alt, a/c and idler in place, but work has been to busy to "play" with it lately.<P>Performance wise. stock 4.9 is around or just over 200 hp and torque is around or just under 290 lbs.  Can't wait till spring!<P>Nice to see all this interest and experience.<P>Pete||||reg||1
Z||000070||Ferrari_cdn||12-18-2001||08:45 PM||||Lots of piping and wiring  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ICEFierosig.gif"> AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000071||rockcrawl||12-18-2001||09:08 PM||||On the G-tech, my 4.5 with 4 speed auto and 2.84 fdr runs 0-60 in 5.2 and 1/4 mi in 14.28 with a 200 lb passenger. It could be faster, I just haven't been able to put much time into tuning it. George has a 4.9 with a manual trans. BubbaJoe? No comment.<P>edited- becauase I felt bad after seeing what I wrote.<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000072||AnimalGT||12-18-2001||09:35 PM||||mrfixit58- Thanks for the info about the Allante intake.<BR>  I know the 4.X motors use 1.6:1 rockers, but I don't believe they use roller tipped rockers, someone correct me if I'm wrong.  Also there are 2 diff 1.6 roller rocker stud sizes that I'm aware of, and I was curoius if anyone knew which size the Caddy engine has. (The sizes are 3/8" and 10mm, IIRC.)<BR> <BR>rockcrawl- Thanks for the info about the springs and rockers.  I was looking for valve spring specs everywhere, but couldn't find it.  How does the rocker mount on the Caddy motors?  Does it have paired keepers like an Olds, studs like a SBC, or a rail?<BR>  Also, what ECM mods have you done?  And how bad is the learing curve for them?<P>  Additionally, I guess I should clarify my statement about "6500 rpm shifts for 50k mile".  What I meant was, can the Caddy motors last for 50k miles of occasional 6500 rpm (redline) manual shifts?  While I think this is probably at the limits of a streetable 4.X, I don't think it's unreasonable with valve train and ECM mods (& probably some porting too).<P>  I know all of this is bench racing at its worst, but I'm trying to get an idea of what can reasonably be done w/ these motors.  Up to 2 weeks ago I was going to put a modded 3.4 in my '85 GT, followed by a turbo from a 3.1 next winter.  (I've got most of the HP parts for the 3.4 already.)  But now, I'm leaning more toward a modded Caddy V-8 build-up.  I also know that I should try a stock 4.X before I mod anything.  But, since the motor would be out of the car already, I'd just as soon do some power enhancing modifications right away.  If I do get a 4.X, I plan on modifying it with a cam, new factory lifters, roller rockers, daul valve springs, mild port and polish, and a modified Chevy ECM.  I phoned Delta Cams last week, and called some yards this week.  I think a Caddy motor w/ these mods could be built for a relatively reasonable price.  I'm just trying to get some info that will help me make an educated decision about which motor to use.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Scott      <BR>  <p>[This message has been edited by AnimalGT (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000073||Will||12-18-2001||10:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B> All it really is, is a lifter kit... Won't solve the problem of the feeble valve-springs, or the ECM-limiter. I can't see it making a hell of a lot of difference. Most hydraulic lifters are good to about ~6000rpm anyway... I'd save the money, and go for a cam... Even *IF* they made these things for a 4.9.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What it does is remove the reciprocating mass of the lifter from the main valve spring. With less reciprocating mass to move, the stock valve spring can control valve motion adequately at a higher RPM. Since the extra spring force is applied directly to the lifter body, it doesn't try to collapse the lifter, and doesn't require a stronger pushrod.||||reg||1
Z||000074||Mach10||12-18-2001||11:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> What it does is remove the reciprocating mass of the lifter from the main valve spring. With less reciprocating mass to move, the stock valve spring can control valve motion adequately at a higher RPM. Since the extra spring force is applied directly to the lifter body, it doesn't try to collapse the lifter, and doesn't require a stronger pushrod.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>My bad, I didn't look closely enough... It's a roller-cam kit... From what I understand, the 4.X are rollers, anyway? Not much of a difference, then...<P>||||reg||11
Z||000075||Slammed Fiero||12-19-2001||12:29 PM||||What do we do about a flywheel?<P>BubbaJoe says to use a FWD cavalier unit, but doesn't say much about making it work with the V8. I assume it has to be rebalanced?<P>Don't really want to go custom flywheel , want to keep it cheap. I picked up a 2.5Ltr Grand Am Alternator this morning , and I think I finally found a home for my B&B Triflow exhaust.<P>JM <p>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 12-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000076||Will||12-19-2001||01:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>What do we do about a flywheel?<P>BubbaJoe says to use a FWD cavalier unit, but doesn't say much about making it work with the V8. I assume it has to be rebalanced?<P>Don't really want to go custom flywheel , want to keep it cheap. I picked up a 2.5Ltr Grand Am Starter this morning , and I think I finally found a home for my B&B Triflow exhaust.<P>JM </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <A HREF="http://www.thefieroshop.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefieroshop.com</A>  has aluminum flywheels for 2.8, 3.1, and 3.8. Demand for these pieces can't be so high that they just have shelves and shelves of them...<BR>Maybe next time they put in an order for flywheels they could run a small batch with a 4.9 bolt circle and a small batch with Northstar bolt circle...||||reg||1
Z||000077||cadero2dmax||12-19-2001||07:01 PM||||Greg Duncan has machined a flywheel specifically for this purpose, and he still has a couple for sale. It is the same flywheel Ed is offering with his 4.9 kit.<P>I have one of these flywheels with my Isuzu 3.35 FDR 5 speed and Centerforce clutch (Greg also has had Centerforce modify a clutch for this swap that has roller bearings).<P>Fun on the street, but for competition, to harness that torque a little better and give better launches, I recommend a 2.84 or 3.06 ratio TH 125.<P>Write me off list at gryan3@mindspring.com for Greg e-mail addy.<P>G<p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 12-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000078||Slammed Fiero||12-20-2001||01:13 AM||||I talked to Greg (email) he has one flywheel left and it's $450. It's Billet aluminum and weighs 15lbs.<P>That may be a real nice addition down the road , but right now I want a cheaper alternative.<P>There must be some way to get around this problem without going to an auto tranny. <P>I'm going to talk to a machinist friend tomorrow to see what his view  is on making me a custom flywheel.<P>Any ideas? Anyone done it without the custom flywheel?<P>Thanks<BR>Jonathan. ||||reg||1
Z||000079||westtexas||12-20-2001||08:23 AM||||<BR>That flywheel is going to be spinning behind your back very fast. If you trust welded metal to stay together at 5,000+ rpm, you could take a Cad flex plate and a neutrally balanced flywheel to a machine shop to have the flywheel welded and redrilled to the Cad bolt pattern. <P>Robert <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000080||Will||12-20-2001||08:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>I talked to Greg (email) he has one flywheel left and it's $450. It's Billet aluminum and weighs 15lbs.<P>...<P>Any ideas? Anyone done it without the custom flywheel?<P>Thanks<BR>Jonathan. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>15 lbs! And its alumerum? Doesn't the stock cast iron unit weigh 16?<P>The thread show pictures of how I modified a neutral balance V6 flywheel: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/012465.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/012465.html</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000081||Slammed Fiero||12-20-2001||10:39 AM||||I was wrong , It's billet steel. Thanks for the flywheel link , this is the type of info I needed. The draw to this conversion is it's cost to power ratio. If I spend $450 on a flywheel I may as well throw out the idea of a "Cheap" conversion. Thats more than most of the motors I have found (Keep in mind I'm canadian , and out dollar is at $1.63 today).<P>JM <p>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000082||rockcrawl||12-20-2001||04:02 PM||||I don't have a problem with welding/plugging and redrilling a flywheel. I'd rather spend that $450 on a camshaft and a set of springs. What do you think WCF uses for their 3800 conversions? Yep, welded and drilled. For a 4.9, start with a neutral flywheel from a FWD V6/60 or an '88 Fiero. You also need a bushing in the center like the one Will used on his northstar. The 4.1 and 4.5 have a wieghted flywheel, but BubbaJoe told me he uses a neutral flywheel with no noticeable vibration  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> I recomend having the new flywheel ballanced to match the old flexplate.||||reg||1
Z||000083||Slammed Fiero||12-20-2001||05:06 PM||||I asked my dad about a welded and drilled flywheel and the safety of it (Long Long time racer). He said he has been using one in the corviar for 20 years now with no problems. That motor sees Higher RPMs than a Northstar..good enough for 8 grand ..good enough for 4800. As long as you have someone with experience do it , it should be fine. The beauty of this swap is the Cost per HP ratio , toss in a $450 flywheel and kiss that theroy goodbye!!<P>$450 X 1.63 Canadain dollar = $733 flywheel...<P>Most 4.9's I have found cost less than that! I can see for team python why they used a unit like that (they are automatic now correct?) But for street / occasional Autocross useage I will take the less expensive road.<P>Rockcrawl check your PM's I sent you one concerning wiring.<P><BR>JM||||reg||1
Z||000084||rockcrawl||12-20-2001||05:53 PM||||Python used a custom flywheel because Greg (1/2 of Python) was able to make it himself. It didn't cost anywhere near $450 for them to use one. I suspect if they did not have the ability to make one, they would have welded and drilled it like the rest of us.||||reg||1
Z||000085||Mach10||12-20-2001||06:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>I don't have a problem with welding/plugging and redrilling a flywheel. I'd rather spend that $450 on a camshaft and a set of springs. What do you think WCF uses for their 3800 conversions? Yep, welded and drilled. For a 4.9, start with a neutral flywheel from a FWD V6/60 or an '88 Fiero. You also need a bushing in the center like the one Will used on his northstar. The 4.1 and 4.5 have a wieghted flywheel, but BubbaJoe told me he uses a neutral flywheel with no noticeable vibration   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> I recomend having the new flywheel ballanced to match the old flexplate.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was under the impression that the Caddy is a fairly well internally balanced motor, anyway... That would explain the neutral-no vibe thing. Also, the more cylinders tend to be smoother... If nothing else, it would be a harmonic vibration, detectable only at 1/2 intervals.... I dunno<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000086||rockcrawl||12-20-2001||08:14 PM||||I don't have a 4.9 flexplate handy to check, but I believe it is internally ballanced. The 4.9 crank has bigger counterweights and the block is machined for clearance. The 4.1 and 4.5 are definately externally ballanced via a weighted flexplate.||||reg||1
